[Music]  hi it's Michelle from Lab muffin Beauty  science here skincare nerd chemistry PhD  and big fan of guacamole with extra  lemon juice lemon is awesome in food but  today I'm going to be talking about the  science of why lemon belongs in your  body and not on your body if you like  this sort of video give it a thumbs up  subscribe to my channel and click the  notification bell to make sure you don't  miss any of my videos if you've ever  been on Pinterest you'll know that  there's tons and tons of DIY skincare  recipes a lot of them use common kitchen  ingredients and one of the most popular  ones is lemon juice  you'll find it in tons of hair and skin  recipes these recipes claim that lemon  juice does tons and tons of different  things it will brighten skin or lighten  pigmentation it'll clear up your  breakouts and reduce oil production  exfoliate your skin and reduce wrinkles  it will get rid of blackheads or reduce  your debt it will solve global warming  lemon is cheap and it smells really nice  but is it effective and more importantly  is that safe let's talk about the  science behind lemon juice let's start  by talking about the chemicals inside  lemon juice there is a bit of truth  about all of these amazing claims about  what lemon juice can do lemon juice does  contain a lot of ingredients that are  used in skincare as the Pinterest DIY  recipes will say lemon contains vitamin  C in the form of ascorbic acid ascorbic  acid is a fantastic ingredient for  evening out pigmentation ascorbic acid  is an antioxidant which prevents free  radical damage and so it can help with  sun damage also known as photo aging and  wrinkles ascorbic acid can also  potentially increase the collagen in  your skin which will make a plumper and  less prone to wrinkling  there's also citric acid which is an  alpha hydroxy acid or a a alpha hydroxy  acids will exfoliate your skin I have  talked about them before in my video on  exfoliation there's also a free  exfoliation guide that I've put together  that you can download if you want to  find out more about exfoliation chemical  exfoliants like alpha hydroxy acids can  with acne and aging they can give you  plumper smoother brighter skin another  one of the components of lemon juice is  niacin which is a form of vitamin b3 you  might know it better in the form of  niacin amide it's an anti-inflammatory  that helps with pigmentation and it  helps with dry skin as well so  theoretically your lemon juice could be  great for your skin but of course  there's a catch  so don't rub a lemon on your face yet an  important question we have to ask is  what is the strength of each of these  ingredients in the lemon juice one of  the problems with natural ingredients is  that there's lots of variation about  what chemicals are actually in there the  exact components will vary with things  like season locations so where it's  grown how fresh the extract is how ripe  it was when it was harvested how it's  been stored  whether it's wim refrigerated or not  whether it's been left in the Sun I've  talked about some of these issues in my  video on natural skincare but let's have  a look at what's in a typical lemon  it's got somewhere around forty four  point five milligrams of ascorbic acid  per hundred mils of lemon juice this  translates to naught point naught four  percent there's about five percent  citric acid and there's naught point  naught naught naught 1 percent niacin if  we compare these percentages to what's  in a formulated skincare product  normally you'll see ascorbic acid and  vitamin C serums at about five to 20  percent alpha hydroxy acids will be true  to fifteen percent and niacin amide will  be two to ten percent so you can see  that the dose of these ingredients in  our lemon juice is pretty low compared  to in a properly formulated skincare  product apart from the citric acid what  this means is that you're not really  getting enough of these ingredients on  your skin through lemon juice to make a  big difference it's not going to be a  great substitute for a regular skincare  product  so in terms of effectiveness lemon juice  is probably not that great  [Music]  we also need to talk about safety  lemons contain millions of other  chemicals apart from these ones that we  know are good in skincare that's because  most things in nature are complex  mixtures  they haven't been purified yet one of  the biggest issues about lemon juice is  that it contains photoshot sick  chemicals these are called saralyn's  which are a subset of furanocoumarins or  fewer coumarins these chemicals are  found in many citrus peels including  lemon and lime if you're squeezing a  lemon at home you can't really separate  the juice from these phototoxic  components these Fiorano coumarins also  found in parsnips celery parsley and  figs the problem is that when they're on  your skin they react with UV to cause  something called Phytophthora dermatitis  in other words a skin reaction caused by  plants when it interacts with sunlight  the symptoms of red skin and horrific  blistering that develops over the next  48 hours after light exposure there's  going to be some really gross blisters  coming up so look away now if you're  squeamish  it's over the blisters are gone oh it's  okay it's okay  it's not entirely clear what the exact  mechanism of how these chemicals caused  this massive blistering on your skin  either they react with DNA took form  cross links or they interact with  proteins in your skin which causes your  keratinocytes or skin cells to die and  your membrane start rupturing the  overall process leads to damage cell  membranes which results in cell death  edema which is swelling blistering and  injury to your skins after this reaction  you might get post inflammatory  hyperpigmentation which is pigmentation  after damage and this can take months to  heal in proper skin care products a lot  of the time the citrus essential oils  have been processed to remove these  photo toxic chemicals but this doesn't  happen if you giy another scary possible  side effect is something called chemical  leuco derma this is when your skin gets  gee pigmented in other words you get  uneven areas of faded pigment there's  been a few reports in the literature of  this chemical leuco journal for example  one woman used a lemon toner for three  months on her face and so you can see  the areas where there's been  hyperpigmentation in other words less  pigment than there should be in another  instance someone rubbed a lemon on their  face for five days their skin became  really irritated it scaled up the skin  exfoliated and then they got lesions  these lesions healed after about five  days but it left gee pigmented patches  this doesn't seem to be an allergic  reaction it's some sort of toxic  reaction and it could be partly because  lemon juice has quite a low pH in other  words it's very acidic it has a pH of  about true  [Music]  so we assumed at the start that lemon  juice was cheap but isn't really in  Australia citric acid is two dollars 25  475 grams  whereas lemons are currently a dollar  seventy eight each each lemon contains  thirty to forty five mils of juice five  percent converts to one point five to  two point two five grams of citric acid  so 75 grams would be sixty to eighty  nine dollars it's actually way cheaper  to go out and buy citric acid and  dissolve it in water than to use lemon  juice plus you avoid the throat Oh  toxicity I still probably wouldn't  recommend it because the pH would still  be quite low so even if you avoided the  light induced blisters you could still  get the hyperpigmentation and citric  acid is quite large so it will work  better in a properly formulated product  designed for skin  if we compare lemon juice with properly  formulated products it isn't that cheap  either  since citric acid isn't that popular in  exfoliants I'm comparing it against  other alpha hydroxy acids like glycolic  and lactic acid and I've converted them  so we're comparing the same number of  molecules of each type of áááá as you  can see if there are some products that  are quite comparable in price plus  there's even a couple that are cheaper  these products have a lot of added  benefits for example a lot of them have  antioxidants plus they're in a base that  will help the alpha hydroxy acids  penetrate and work now there's the  concept that the dose makes the poison  so lemon juice might not always be  unsafe but I wouldn't recommend using it  and leave on recipes with large amounts  of lemon juice in wash off recipes or  recipes were really small amounts of  lemon juice it might be fine it's still  a lot safer to use a product that's been  specifically formulated to go on your  skin if you're still determined to use  lemon juice then I highly recommend that  you only use wash-off recipes with  really small amounts of lemon juice and  to immediately stop if you get  irritation or if you see any pigment  changes you should also wash it off  really thoroughly before you go out into  the Sun and use sunscreen I hope you  enjoyed this video if you liked it  please give it a thumbs up and subscribe  to my channel  more videos like this you can also  follow me on Instagram and check out my  blog for tons more beauty science see  you next time  you

